    "content": "An autopen is a mechanical device, often a robotic arm, used to replicate a person’s signature using a real pen or marker. Patented in 1803, these machines allow for mass signing of documents like letters, photos, and official papers. They are commonly used by government officials and US presidents. Key Aspects of Autopens: Usage: They provide high-quality signature replication, often used when an individual, such as a president or university president, needs to sign thousands of items. Mechanism: Modern machines can use digital signatures to control a pen or marker. Presidential Usage: Numerous US presidents, including Eisenhower, Kennedy, Obama, Trump, and Biden, have utilized autopens to sign documents, including, at times, legislation and correspondence. Legality: According to the Justice Department, a president does not need to personally sign a bill for it to become law. Detection: Autopen signatures often exhibit shakiness, jagged areas, or unnatural breaks due to machine vibrations, often referred to as \"machine tremor\". The technology has been a topic of debate, with criticisms arising when used for high-level government actions like signing bills or pardons. While the use of an autopen itself is not illegal, questions sometimes arise about the oversight of the process.",
